{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790731300,"nonce":"90yCoPNmcoVgX/3QwJ908ClXaxI=","title":"Hybrid presets leave a large physics throughput gap after tier warmup","message":"ChimpMonk's island solver benchmark passes its serial-versus-worker state check, but Ash hybrid remains much slower than forced JIT and AOT on the same dense scene. This is a throughput and tier-coverage issue, separate from the earlier Single-precision trajectory defect.\n\nReproducer: ChimpMonk commit 7ccf4b7b4115d9a6138d7a73c8a18fc093f8bacb (the later .gitignore-only commit does not affect it), Ash commit 1657de66d3ac24643e1cd6e3a0a92cc905219b5a. From the ChimpMonk root on macOS arm64:\n\n    haxe -cp . -cp example/src -cp ../ash/haxelib/ash-simd -D ash_simd -main IslandParallelChecks -hl /tmp/island-preset.hl\n    ASH_WORKERS=4 ../ash/target/debug/ash --preset benchmark /tmp/island-preset.hl 8 64 20 128 4 distance\n    ASH_WORKERS=4 ../ash/target/debug/ash --mode jit --jit-tier cranelift /tmp/island-preset.hl 8 64 20 128 4 distance\n    ../ash/target/debug/ash --build /tmp/island-preset-aot /tmp/island-preset.hl\n    ASH_WORKERS=4 /tmp/island-preset-aot 8 64 20 128 4 distance\n\nThe arguments mean eight separate 64-box joint-connected stacks, 20 frames, 128 constraints per island gate, four workers. The check prints the time spent in World.step for serial and parallel copies and verifies body-state agreement. A recent cold run gave 6949/6363 ms under the benchmark hybrid preset versus 953/708 ms with forced Cranelift JIT. Repeated AOT runs gave medians near 181/174 ms. These are machine-specific timings; the large hybrid/JIT gap, rather than a preset ranking, is the concern.\n\nA second control first steps both a 16x8 serial world and a 16x8 worker world for 120 frames, drops them, runs a major GC, then times fresh 8x64 worlds. Under game and application presets the fresh scene is about 3.1 seconds in each solver mode; benchmark is also about 3.1 seconds in repeated runs. The same warm protocol under --mode jit --jit-tier cranelift takes about 0.25/0.23 seconds, and forced LLVM JIT about 0.28/0.37 seconds. An ASH_TIER_LOG=1 benchmark run still installs Cranelift functions during the fresh measured scene, with install timestamps around 8.6, 10.6, and 15.8 seconds. This suggests some hot reached functions have not been promoted by the warmup; the exact cause needs isolation.\n\nPlease profile which physics functions remain interpreted or promote late under presets, why they miss the tiering thresholds or call-path accounting, and whether the game preset can avoid compilation during active frames. Compare warmed hybrid throughput against JIT and AOT without forcing a mode in application code. The ChimpMonk state checks pass in all modes; this issue concerns speed and frame timing.","files":null}]}